{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And what shall I do for these daughters today</b> — They said (Ibn Ezra and Radak) that it is like 'and what shall I do for them today,' and it is an added explanation. And similarly, 'which we two have sworn, we two' (I Samuel 20:42), 'our sons and our daughters, we' (Nehemiah 5:2). But it appears more correct that it is an expression of compassion: 'And what shall I do for these daughters who are before me,' for my compassion was stirred for them, or for their children who were born in my house and they are to me like my sons — and this is the meaning of 'that they bore.' He said this as one defending himself against Jacob's words: 'I came after you to see my daughters, and what can I do for them or for their children that is good? And now I will do them good — that you make a covenant with me, that you shall not afflict them nor take other wives upon them.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:30.802106691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}